{"kind":"request","text":"Builder: container sizing and operator observability (simplification review 55563589, findings COST-03, COST-10, COST-11). (1) The Publisher container starts with no instance type and no max_instances (git/src/publisher/container.ts:152-153; room/wrangler.spike.jsonc:54-64), one per room (config.ts:57), with a 15-minute inactivity timeout that the publication loop never lets expire. Pin instance_type lite and max_instances, and shorten the inactivity timeout once the loop is fixed. (2) There are no console calls, no Analytics Engine and no metrics in room, git or checkers production paths; room.ts:190 and core.ts:1652 swallow step failures; jobTokenDuties and tick are not routed. An operator cannot see alarm cadence per room, publication count and failures, job attempts and container seconds, Artifacts operations, fork counts, open duties, unknown mints, or how many rooms exist and who founded them. Add structured log lines or Analytics Engine data points at alarm, publish, issue and refusal, with the room ID and the reason, and an operator read for duties and room inventory (the stale request c0f0592f covers the duties read; supersede or revive it). (3) Long polls up to 60 s and RPC subscribe loops keep the Room awake and billed for duration (room.ts:122-165; client default 25 s; MCP waitMs up to 300 s), about $4 per room-month per continuous poller, while the hibernatable WebSocket path exists (room.ts:284). Cap waitMs and RPC stream lifetime and steer clients to the WebSocket.","body":{"conditions":"wrangler configs pin instance type and caps; the four event kinds emit structured records with a test; the operator read exists over HTTPS and RPC with a test; waitMs capped with a test; root gates pass; checker review.","target_head":"a6330262b1a609843308d9a493dbd6132d428499","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}